#### Useful flags for NMAP for me to reference later if needed ###

#Find active hosts with# 

-sn

you can also specfify the subnet

#### Looks for all 255 hosts that may be up in that subnet ####

Ex: nmap -sn 192.168.1.1/24


#List all scanable targets with#

-sL

This only lists scanable targets, it does not actually scan them.

# How to check for open ports #

-sT

nmap will try to complete a 3-way handshake with every TCP port.

# Stealth Scans #

-sS

Only sends SYN packet, never completes the handshake.

# Scanning UDP ports #

-sU

# Fast scan #

-F

Only scans 100 ports instead of 1,000

# Specifying ports / port range #

-p[range]

EX: nmap 192.168.1.1 -p10-1024

# Detecting Operating System #

-o

Not super accurate

# Service and Version Detection #

-sV

# Want OS detection and Service/Version Detection? #

-A 

WABAM!

# Want to scan down hosts? #

-Pn

WABAM!

# Controlling scan speed #

-T0 for paranoid
-T1 for sneaky
-T2 for polite
-T3 for normal 
-T4 for aggressive

# Controlling number of Parallel Probes #

--min-parallelism <amount>
--max-parallelism <amount>

# Control packets per second #

--min-rate<number>
--max-rate<number>

# Maximum Wait time for hosts #

--host--timeout

# Verbosity #

-v -vv -vv -vvvv

	or 

-v1 -v2 -v3 -v4

# Debug mode (SO MUCH STUFF) #

-d

# Saving Results #

-oN <name of file> (normal output)
-oX <file> (XML output)
